\section*{Summary}

\begin{shaded}

\noindent Intensive monitoring in 2025 of the only known Awak\=opaka skink population has generated sufficient information for decision making. Monitoring sessions during February and December captured a total of nine skinks including three females. The females were subsequently removed to be part of a captive breeding programme. The removal of the females violated the assumption population closure required by variable detection mark-recapture, occupancy, and N-mixture modelling techniques. However, as no males were removed, estimates could be made for the male component of the population. The chance repeated recapture of a single male skink across monitoring periods has enabled mark-recapture estimates to be produced (a situation previously thought unlikely). Based on our analysis it appears the monitored population possibly contains fewer than 20 males (with mean estimates of 10 and 14 males from the February and December monitoring). While there is relatively high uncertainty, Monte Carlo estimation suggests that, on average, 90\% of the population estimates would fall below 34 males, and 95\% would fall below 53. Given no other populations are known and the study site constitutes the majority of the only known population, the Awak\=opaka skink appear to sit on the very edge of extinction. The removal of the few available females for captive breeding purposes undoubtedly increases that risk. Furthermore, the population's low detection probability (\mytilde 0.05) when combined with its low population suggests that even if an effective \emph{in situ} intervention could be deployed the ability to detect a population response will be severely hampered. Under such circumstances the viability of future \emph{in situ} conservation for this species should be reviewed.



\end{shaded}


\section*{Background}

Awak\=opaka skinks were discovered in 2014 from a small area of rugged boulder habitat near the Homer Saddle. Fewer than than 20 individuals were located prior to 2025. Surveying in 2024 using Gee's minnow traps only detected four individuals from 208 trap nights (over 5 days of surveying). 

A comprehensive analysis of options for the monitoring designs (Whitmore 2024) determined that a nested spatial approach be employed in order to allow for the possible use of a large suite of statistical methods (including occupancy methods, N-mixture models, variable detection mark-recapture, and count based indices). Mark-recapture methods were considered to be at a high risk of failure given a low detection rate. As a result, a target of 10 monitoring sessions per monitoring period was recommended. 


\section*{Methods}

Mark recapture data was analysed in program MARK via the Robust Design under the assumption of no migration using a Huggins variant. As the data only contained data from six male individuals, with only one of those individuals being recaptured (twice), only a single, simple, model \emph{S(.)p(.)=c(.)} was considered. As under the Huggins method abundance is treated as a derived variable two estimates are produced (one for each monitoring period). As the resultant estimates were somewhat dissimilar a Monte Carlo approach was utilised to produce a distribution of the average abundance estimate using 100,000 draws based on the model's beta coefficients.

\section*{Results}

\subsection*{Mark-recapture}

The singular model \emph{S(.)p(.)=c(.)} returned an abundance estimate for males of 10 (95\% CI: 4--40) for the February sampling and 14 (95\% CI: 6--55) for the December sampling. Male survival was uninformative with insufficient encounter histories to generate a meaningful estimate. The detection probability was very low (mean = 0.04, 95\% CI: 0.01--0.13). Abundance averaging led to a median estimate of 12 males, a 90th percentile of 33 males, and a 95th percentile of 53 males (Figure \ref{fig:percentile}). 


