\section*{Discussion}

It appears that the current monitoring intensity is minimally sufficient to generate an abundance estimate due to multiple recaptures of a single animal. This has allowed population estimates for to be generated for males at the study site. The two monitoring sessions conducted thus far have produced similar estimates of 10 and 14 males, indicating that population is likely small albeit with a high degree of uncertainty demonstrated by large confidence intervals. The estimated capture/recapture probably at 0.04 (95\% CI: 0.01 -- 0.13) is likely the lowest recorded among New Zealand lizards. Such a low detection probability can be expected hamper efforts to track the population trajectory over time. Efforts to improve detection probability through the used of baited Gee's Minnow traps are likely to be trialed in the forthcoming monitoring period (Megan Willans pers. comms.). If we assume the estimates are accurate, and there is sex parity then a simple doubling of the estimates might suggest that around 24 animals might be within the study site boundary. As the study site is the only known stronghold of the species the low population estimate suggests they are in immediate danger of extinction.

While home range information is limited to a single recaptured male, this individual appeared to show site fidelity, occupying a small area of the study site on its northern boundary. Its recorded path length of 38.4m over three recaptures suggests this individual likely had a comparatively small and stable home range. 

